  "lines": [
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Dad, why does ice float?",
      "board": {
        "type": "title",
        "left": {
          "icon": "🧊",
          "label": "Ice cube"
        },
        "right": {
          "icon": "🥤",
          "label": "Floats?"
        },
        "mid": "?"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "Great question! Look in your drink. The ice cubes bob at the top, instead of sinking to the bottom.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🥤",
        "text": "Ice bobs on top!",
        "cue": "bob",
        "sub": "It doesn't sink",
        "sub_cue": "sinking"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "But ice is hard. Shouldn't it be heavy and sink?"
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "Here's the secret. When water freezes, it spreads out and takes up more space.",
      "punch": "space",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🧊",
        "text": "Freezing water spreads out",
        "cue": "spreads",
        "sub": "It takes more space",
        "sub_cue": "space"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Wait, so ice is bigger than the water it came from?"
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "A little bit, yes! In water, the tiny bits are squished close. In ice, they lock together in a pattern with little gaps.",
      "board": {
        "type": "compare",
        "left": {
          "label": "Water",
          "items": [
            {
              "text": "Squished close",
              "icon": "💧",
              "cue": "squished"
            }
          ]
        },
        "right": {
          "label": "Ice",
          "items": [
            {
              "text": "Locked pattern",
              "icon": "🧊",
              "cue": "lock"
            },
            {
              "text": "Little gaps",
              "icon": "🕳️",
              "cue": "gaps"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "So those gaps make ice lighter?"
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "Exactly! Ice is lighter for its size than water, so the water pushes it up and it floats.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "⬆️",
        "text": "Lighter for its size",
        "cue": "lighter",
        "sub": "So it floats!",
        "sub_cue": "floats"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Does everything get bigger when it freezes?"
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "Nope! Most things get smaller when they freeze. Water is special, because it gets bigger!",
      "punch": "special",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "⭐",
        "text": "Water is special!",
        "cue": "special",
        "sub": "It gets bigger!",
        "sub_cue": "bigger"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "And that helps animals! In winter, ice floats on top of ponds like a lid, and fish stay safe in the water below.",
      "board": {
        "type": "gallery",
        "title": "Ice lid",
        "items": [
          {
            "icon": "❄️",
            "label": "Winter",
            "cue": "winter"
          },
          {
            "icon": "🧊",
            "label": "Ice on top",
            "cue": "ice"
          },
          {
            "icon": "🐟",
            "label": "Fish below",
            "cue": "fish"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "So ice has gaps, it's lighter, and it floats. Cool!",
      "board": {
        "type": "summary",
        "rows": [
          {
            "text": "Ice has gaps",
            "icon": "🧊",
            "cue": "gaps"
          },
          {
            "text": "Lighter for its size",
            "icon": "🪶",
            "cue": "lighter"
          },
          {
            "text": "So it floats!",
            "icon": "⬆️",
            "cue": "floats"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "DAD",
      "text": "Very cool! Ask a grown-up to subscribe to The Curio Kids Club for more wonders! And next time someone asks... why does ice float?"
